{"from": "site:capernaum-synagogue", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:lk-07-05-built-synagogue", "grade": "C", "note": "Beneath the 4th-5th century synagogue at Capernaum lies a basalt foundation that Loffreda suggests is a 1st-century synagogue, perhaps the one in the Gospels: a synagogue stood at Capernaum, and synagogue-building is plausible there. Graded C, not B, because the 1st-century dating is a scholarly suggestion and the site cannot confirm the patron."}
{"from": "site:capernaum-synagogue", "type": "complicates", "to": "claim:lk-07-05-built-synagogue", "note": "The visible synagogue was built around the 4th or 5th century; whether the basalt foundation beneath it is the 1st-century synagogue rests on Loffreda's suggestion, so no standing structure can be identified as the building the centurion funded."}
{"from": "artifact:theodotos-inscription", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:lk-07-05-built-synagogue", "grade": "C", "note": "The inscription commemorates the construction of a synagogue complex by Theodotos, son of Vettenus, in 1st-century (Herodian or early Roman) Jerusalem: a named individual building a synagogue is attested practice. It is from Jerusalem, not Capernaum, and does not name the centurion."}
{"from": "site:nein", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:lk-07-11-nain-city", "grade": "C", "note": "Nein preserves the ancient name (also Nain or Naim in English) and is an ancient settlement at the right location - near Endor, at the foot of the Hill of Moreh, south of Mount Tabor - with rock-sunk tombs; the tombs are 'probably of Christian origin', so the site evidence is contextual."}
{"from": "text:eusebius-onomasticon-nain", "type": "attests", "to": "place:nain", "grade": "D", "note": "The 4th-century Onomasticon locates Naim as a village south of (Mount) Thabor near Endor and reports the raising of the widow's son there: it names the same place, but it is a reception text whose story depends on the Gospel; its geographic report is its own. Grade D per the reception rule."}
{"from": "text:mishnah-ketubot-4-5", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:lk-07-12-funeral-procession", "grade": "B", "note": "Even the poorest man may not provide fewer than two flutes and a lamenting woman for a funeral: hired public mourning was customary and expected. Redacted c. 200 CE, so it may not describe the practice of the 30s CE exactly. Regraded C to B per the architect's Mishnah ruling (2026-11-15): the Mishnah names the same practice the claim states."}
{"from": "text:pliny-nh-13-unguents", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:lk-07-37-alabaster-ointment", "grade": "C", "note": "Pliny's book on unguents shows oil-based perfumes were a major Roman industry and the alabaster flask their standard container (reused from Matthew 26): the object the woman brings is the normal luxury vessel of the period."}
{"from": "text:pliny-nh-12-nard", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:lk-07-37-alabaster-ointment", "grade": "C", "note": "Pliny prices pure nard at 100 denarii a pound: costly perfumed ointment was a known expensive commodity. Luke does not name nard, so this attests the class of costly ointment, not the woman's specific unguent."}
{"from": "artifact:denarius", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:lk-07-41-two-debtors", "grade": "C", "note": "The denarius was the standard Roman silver day-wage coin (reuse from Mark 6 and Matthew 20): 500 denarii is about 20 months' and 50 about 2 months' wages for a laborer. Attests the coin and wage scale the parable presupposes, not the parable's figures."}
{"from": "text:pliny-nh-13-unguents", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:lk-07-44-46-hospitality", "grade": "C", "note": "Oil-based perfumes for the body were standard grooming in the Roman world: anointing a guest's head with oil fits the period's customs (the courtesy itself is not independently documented)."}
{"from": "text:josephus-ant-20-theudas", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:lk-07-16-great-prophet", "grade": "C", "note": "Josephus (Ant. 20.97-99, c. 45 CE) records Theudas, who 'told them he was a prophet', persuading 'a great part of the people' to follow him to the Jordan: prophet-figures drawing popular crowds were a living phenomenon of the era. It attests the expectation-context, not the acclamation at Nain; Theudas is later (c. 45 CE) and in Judea."}
{"from": "artifact:second-temple-ossuaries", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:lk-07-12-funeral-procession", "grade": "C", "note": "Secondary burial in ossuaries was one of the varied Second Temple-period Jewish burial customs (reused from Matthew 26): the dead of a Galilean town would be buried with public rites, as the procession presupposes."}
{"from": "site:nein", "type": "identified_as", "to": "place:nain", "note": "Identification of Nein with Luke's Nain recognized by Eusebius and Jerome, by the Crusaders (who built a church there) and by modern geographers; it rests on name-preservation, tradition and topography, not on excavation."}
{"from": "site:nein", "type": "documented_by", "to": "source:wikipedia-nein"}
{"from": "site:nein", "type": "documented_by", "to": "source:wikipedia-nein-tombs"}
