{"from": "claim:lk-07-03-elders-intercede", "type": "related_to", "to": "claim:mt-08-05-centurion-capernaum", "note": "Luke tells the centurion's approach through messengers: Jewish elders (7:3-4) and later friends (7:6); Matthew 8:5-8 has the centurion come and speak to Jesus in person. Luke's servant is 'sick and at the point of death'; Matthew's is paralyzed."}
{"from": "claim:lk-07-37-alabaster-ointment", "type": "related_to", "to": "claim:mt-26-07-costly-ointment", "note": "The same object custom, different occasions: Luke 7 is an unnamed sinful woman in a Pharisee's house in Galilee during the ministry, anointing Jesus's feet; Matthew 26 is in Bethany at Simon the leper's house in the final week, anointing his head. The fetched Anointing of Jesus article records 'considerable debate' over identity, location and timing."}
{"from": "claim:lk-07-36-simon-dinner", "type": "related_to", "to": "claim:mt-26-06-simon-leper-house", "note": "Different Simons and settings: Simon the Pharisee of a Galilean town (Luke 7) against Simon the leper of Bethany (Matthew 26); the occasions and weeks differ."}
{"from": "claim:lk-07-14-raised-widows-son", "type": "related_to", "to": "text:1-kings-17-17-24", "note": "The Nain account echoes Elijah raising the widow of Zarephath's son (the fetched Raising article notes the parallels, some verbal); a biblical cross-reference, not evidence."}
{"from": "claim:lk-07-14-raised-widows-son", "type": "related_to", "to": "text:2-kings-4-18-37", "note": "The Nain account also echoes Elisha raising the Shunammite woman's son, including the people's reaction; Nain lies close to Shunem (fetched Raising article); a biblical cross-reference, not evidence."}
{"from": "claim:lk-07-44-46-hospitality", "type": "related_to", "to": "text:genesis-18-4", "note": "Water brought to wash the guests' feet is the classic hospitality courtesy (Abraham at Mamre); a biblical cross-reference, not evidence."}
{"from": "claim:lk-07-16-great-prophet", "type": "related_to", "to": "text:deuteronomy-18-15-18", "note": "The crowd's acclaim of 'a great prophet' presupposes the expectation of a prophet like Moses; a biblical cross-reference, not evidence."}
